		{
			description: [
				"To mute a Gregtech machine, right-click it with a &6Hammer&r. (Not the Mining kind!)"
				""
				"For other sounds, there is a button in the &etop-left&r of your inventory that looks like a speaker. Clicking it brings you to the &bExtreme Sound Muffler&r menu."
				""
				"In this menu you can &eattenuate the volume of sounds that were recently played&r, or set an \"Anchor\" to reduce the volume of sounds in that area - most of the controls in this menu have a tooltip to tell you what they do."
			]
			icon: {
				Count: 1
				id: "ftbquests:custom_icon"
				tag: {
					Icon: "gtceu:textures/gui/overlay/tool_sound.png"
				}
			}
			id: "3A261E2A934EFAFF"
			size: 1.5d
			subtitle: "My poor ears!"
			tasks: [{
				id: "7C979978ACC70F8E"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "&9Muting and Muffling"
			x: 0.0d
			y: 0.375d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A machine's &amaximum recipe tier&r determines what recipes it can run. Under normal circumstances, the maximum recipe tier of a machine is equivalent to the machine tier for singleblock machines, or the tier of the energy hatch for multiblock machines."
				""
				"You may have noticed that you will need to run an &bMV&r recipe in your &3EBF&r to obtain &6Aluminium&r. Unfortunately, at this point you only have access to &7LV&r energy hatches."
				""
				"To get around this, you will need to raise the maximum recipe tier of your EBF higher than normal using the &eover-tiering&r mechanic."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Most multiblocks can have up to two &6Energy Hatches&r in their structure. The Energy Hatches you have access to are able to take in &a2 Amps&r of power each, as noted in their descriptions."
				""
				"Placing two &7LV&r &6Energy Hatches&r on your EBF lets you power the machine with total of four &7LV&r (32EU/t) amps, worth one &bMV&r (128EU/t) amp. This raises the maximum recipe tier of the EBF to &bMV&r, allowing it to run &bMV&r-tier recipes."
				""
				"[\"\",{\"text\":\"Raising the maximum recipe tier this way can result in &e&ooverclocking&r, which is a distinct mechanic not to be confused with over-tiering. You can read more about it \"},{\"underlined\":\"true\",\"text\":\"here\",\"clickEvent\":{\"action\":\"change_page\",\"value\":\"31AE366B15538079\"}},{\"text\":\".\"}]"
			]
			icon: "gtceu:lv_voltage_coil"
			id: "4AE1FF69AF05E45E"
			require_sequential_tasks: false
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.25d
			subtitle: "Not to be confused with overclocking"
			tasks: [{
				id: "27876582D407D044"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "Recipe Tiers and Over-tiering"
			x: 3.25d
			y: 3.75d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"When a machine is operating at a higher recipe tier than what is required for running the current recipe, it will &aoverclock&r (&aOC&r) the recipe. Each overclock on a recipe will halve its duration but quadruple the rate of energy consumption. For example, a 30 EU/t recipe running in an &bMV&r machine will run at double speed but require 120EU/t."
				""
				"The only reason not to run recipes at higher speed is the additional power cost. If this is an issue, you might want to build more lower tier machines in place of overclocking."
				""
				"Additionally, &8ULV&r recipes will not overclock to &7LV&r. A ≤8 EU/t recipe won't overclock at all in an &7LV&r machine, and will only overclock once in an &bMV&r machine, etc."
				""
				"Overclocks can be visualized with the help of &bEMI&r by interacting with the voltage tier indicator in the bottom right of machine recipes."
			]
			icon: "minecraft:clock"
			id: "2FFDB911731FEB2D"
			subtitle: "Not to be confused with over-tiering"
			tasks: [{
				id: "330E15A0D265BCAE"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "Overclocking"
			x: 1.5d
			y: 3.75d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["1229234A71CE0A99"]
			description: [
				"For now, the best material to make these pipes out of is probably &6Tin&r."
				""
				"Their advantage over &bPipez&r is their far higher &6throughput&r. An unupgraded Item Pipe transports &916 items per second&r, and up to &9256 per second&r when upgraded. In comparison, small low-tier Item Pipes can transfer &964 items per second&r, and those made from later materials can transfer &9dozens of stacks per second&r."
				""
				"They don't auto-extract by themselves, so you have to use &3Conveyor Covers&r etc. with them."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"The priority mechanics are as follows:"
				""
				"A Destination will have a Routing Value calculated, which is the sum of all Routing Values of the individual Pipes to that Destination. Whichever Destination has the lowest Routing Value will be the one selected for Insertion."
				"Restrictive Pipes typically have the lowest Priority for insertion due to their higher Routing Value."
			]
			id: "571FE20AF5D8F124"
			rewards: [{
				count: 2
				id: "67E64D1EE0CCC181"
				item: "ulvcovm:ulv_conveyor_module"
				type: "item"
			}]
			shape: "hexagon"
			subtitle: "&2GT Item Pipes will transport items instantly through them and accept GT covers."
			tasks: [{
				icon: "gtceu:tin_normal_item_pipe"
				id: "6CB15B88AA69AB9A"
				item: {
					Count: 1
					id: "itemfilters:id_regex"
					tag: {
						value: "^gtceu:[\\w_]+_item_pipe$"
					}
				}
				title: "Any GT Item Pipe"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&2GT Item Pipes"
			x: 10.5d
			y: -5.75d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"1229234A71CE0A99"
				"0FF18D13CD42F48A"
			]
			description: [
				"&2GT Fluid Pipes&r are quite powerful. Their advantage is high &9throughput&r and the ability to use &9GT covers&r, but they have limitations on the kinds of fluids (&eacids, gases&r, etc) they can transport safely. Check each pipe material's tooltip for details. Fluid pipes do require a pump to be able to pull fluids from tanks or machines that lack an auto-output function, but &2ULV pumps (as well as other covers) are very cheap, and buyable with Monicoins&r."
				""
				"There are also &6Quadruple and Nonuple Pipes&r for transporting multiple fluid types through the same block."
				""
				"You also have access to &bPipez&r, which are cheaper and easier to use"
			]
			id: "7D59E5905949C396"
			rewards: [{
				count: 2
				id: "6CF0112603C1D612"
				item: "ulvcovm:ulv_electric_pump"
				type: "item"
			}]
			tasks: [{
				icon: "gtceu:potin_large_fluid_pipe"
				id: "075779A039222C28"
				item: {
					Count: 1
					id: "itemfilters:id_regex"
					tag: {
						value: "^gtceu:[\\w_]+_fluid_pipe$"
					}
				}
				title: "Any GT Fluid Pipe"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&9GT Fluid Pipes"
			x: 10.25d
			y: -4.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["6197C51E04761371"]
			description: [
				"The &6Machine Controller&r is a cover that manages the operation of &bGregTech&r machines, similar to a &aSoft Mallet&r. These are crucial for &epassive automation&r, where a machine is dedicated to keeping something in stock for you."
				""
				"\"Machines\" here is broader than you might think. It of course works on single-block machines, but can also be used on &eMultiblock Controllers&r like your &3Electronic Blast Furnace&r. But it's even more flexible than that: you can use it on any &bGregTech&r block that accepts transfer covers, as long as the controllable cover is placed on it first! This means it also works for things like &6Chests&r, &6Drums&r, and Multiblock inputs."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"The cover works by detecting a &eredstone signal&r. By default, the entire machine will be disabled if a sufficiently strong signal is detected by the cover."
				""
				"When you open the Machine Controller's GUI with a &aScrewdriver&r, you will see several options for configuring its behavior:"
				""
				"The strength of the signal required to trigger the Machine Controller is configurable using the top slider."
				""
				"Another button allows you to select whether to control the whole machine (where applicable) or a specific cover. This lets you do things like stop moving items into a machine, but also allow the machine to continue working."
				""
				"Switching from &eNormal&r to &eInverted&r mode will make the Machine Controller instead disable the machine until it receives the requisite redstone signal. This is often preferable when working with &6Level Emitters&r to prevent machines from starting up if your &eME Network&r is offline."
			]
			id: "1CB14AC299A8EDD9"
